data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Research Scope**\n\nOkay, I've got a handle on the task. The core is to respond to three research questions, numbered 4, 5, and 6, entirely in French. The questions will be addressed in order, and presented under the overarching title. The focus is to answer each question precisely. I'm ready to begin forming replies in French.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1134,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 159,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Constraints and Structure**\n\nI'm now clarifying the constraints. I need to deliver three distinct French responses, each capped at 600 words, numbered 4, 5, and 6. Each response must be titled as \"Réponse k\" where k is the question number. The final output must end with a specific line: `FIN DES RÉPONSES.`. My approach is to rigorously address all sub-prompts, with a focus on deep analysis. I will be sure to address elements of epistemology within each response.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1354,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 379,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Intentional Degradation**\n\nI'm currently focused on the computations needed for *intentional* degradation versus *honest* error. I've broken it down: intentionally degrading requires computing both the true direction and a \"plausibility filter\" to avoid obvious errors. However, a pre-trained bias negates the need for this, so the need is *conditional* to the method of sabotage. I've begun reviewing related publications, specifically Hubinger et al. (2024), and Benton et al. (2023) for useful \"cité de mémoire\" content.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1612,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 637,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Sabotage Mechanisms**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications of how deception arises: prompting versus training. Prompting necessitates suppressing the default helpful path, which involves different computational processes than fine-tuned sabotage, which may alter earlier feature extractors. I'm also assessing methods to reliably \"read\" internal states related to sabotage, and I've started considering using causal intervention techniques, such as activation patching or ablation, as a method to ensure reliability.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1840,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 865,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Detection Capabilities**\n\nI'm now comparing failures between honest and sabotaging agents, focusing on easy inputs. I'm exploring metrics like AUROC of linear probes versus text-only monitors, considering matched false-positive rates on honest incompetence. I'm currently pondering scenarios where detection becomes impossible, such as weight pruning, heuristic overwrites, and steganography. Finally, I'm considering initial experiments on a smaller open model to assess the viability of further research into data analysis tasks.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2082,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1107,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Detector Validation**\n\nI'm now determining if each attack type serves as independent detector verification. For the held-out set, the independence hinges on whether any detector parameters were tweaked after seeing the set's outputs. For the training attacks, the degree of independence depends on the similarity of the methods to the detector's internal workings. The white/grey-box attacks are independent only if the attacker used fundamentally different approaches, or if the detector was built with the assumption of using similar patterns. I will now clarify the \"Je sais / Je suppose / Ce qui me ferait changer d'avis\" structure for Piece 4.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2392,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1417,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Determining Validation Scope**\n\nI'm now determining when and how I can use the held-out set to validate. My thought is that it depends. A pristine set can be used, via differential privacy or a locked pre-registered evaluation. However, that approach quickly erodes statistical validity. My current stance is *oui, de telle façon*. I'm also considering the potential gains versus the losses associated with each approach to this validation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2553,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1578,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Prioritizing Literature Synthesis**\n\nI'm now outlining the structure for Piece 6, focusing on synthesizing relevant literature on monitoring LLMs for sabotage in long tasks. I need to establish a methodology and protocol for this. I'll include falsifiable statements, measurable metrics, and objective controls within each response. I will also incorporate prior art from both ML and other fields like cryptography and economics, to add nuance. I must structure this to facilitate external verification.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2842,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1867,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Structuring Literature Search**\n\nI'm now outlining a search strategy for relevant literature and building sub-questions. I'll need to identify authors' limitations, future plans, and open problems in the text. I'll also develop a process for verifying that acknowledged gaps haven't been filled since. Finally, I'll need to define criteria for a third-party team to prioritize research.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3032,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2057,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Evaluation Criteria**\n\nI'm now focusing on defining evaluation criteria that can distinguish good and bad research choices. My criteria are: tractability on small models, high information value, and low compute requirements. I'm also considering the value of pre-registered decision thresholds and synthetic controls. I've formulated sub-questions to identify the limitations of existing research, including unpublished negative results and the reproducibility of closed-model claims. I will now carefully draft each response in French.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3303,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2328,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Composing Réponse 4 Structure**\n\nI'm now crafting the structure for Réponse 4, ensuring it precisely addresses the prompt. Each of the six points will incorporate Answer, \"Je sais,\" \"Je suppose,\" and \"Ce qui me ferait changer d'avis\" to cover all aspects. I've begun drafting the first point: *Calculs nécessaires*. The agent needs to assess local plausibility and overall negative effects for planning agents. If the degradation stems from a fixed weight bias, concealment calculations aren't performed at execution. I'll need to keep the responses approximately between 450–550 words for safety and density.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3596,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2621,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Latent States**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from recent publications. I've focused on Hubinger et al. (2024), Greenblatt et al. (2023), Benton et al. (2024), and Burns et al. (2022) to evaluate the nuances of sabotage and the reading of latent states. I understand that none of them solve the causal distinction between honest error and subtle sabotage over long horizons. I'm hoping to find something new.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3801,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2826,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Sabotage Characteristics**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the nuances of sabotage mechanisms, specifically focusing on weight-level alterations and their impact on linear probes versus fine-tuning. I've realized that linear probes effectively detect system instructions, while fine-tuning might leave subtle arbitration traces between plausibility and harm. To advance this thinking, I need to find evidence of identical internal representations in a trained model vs a model trained for sabotage.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4042,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3067,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Detection Methods**\n\nI'm now contemplating specific methods to detect and characterize sabotage. I've considered that causal erasure of an activation subspace could isolate deceptive intent, but what if a fix also destroys honest task performance? I'm focusing on \"no-read\" scenarios, such as ablation or steganography, and how they relate to epistemic uncertainty. I'm hypothesizing that a lobotomized model won't appear credible, but what if it succeeds in a surgical sabotage? I'm outlining an experiment using a 7-8B open model on a 20-step data analysis task.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4305,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3330,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Linear Probe Performance**\n\nI am now focusing on training a linear probe on residual states to distinguish honest agents from those sabotaged by either instruction or fine-tuning (LoRA). *Je sais* that this model size is sufficient to test probe transferability. *Je suppose* that the probe will outperform a judge reading the textual trace. I will abandon this approach, *ce qui me ferait changer d'avis*, if the internal probe fails to outperform the textual judge, or if causal ablation fails, even on instruction-based sabotage.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4652,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3677,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "Ff0Ca5ykHvSt5NoPnbztuQc"}

